const faq = [
  { q: 'What do young ravens do after leaving the nest?', a: 'After fledging at approximately 5–6 weeks, young ravens remain with their parents for several weeks to months, learning foraging skills and territory geography. They are then evicted from the parental territory — typically before or during the next breeding season. After eviction, young ravens join communal flocks of other non-breeding birds (floaters), roosting together and foraging communally. They typically spend 2–3 years as floaters before acquiring a mate and territory.' },
  { q: 'How far do young ravens travel from their birthplace?', a: 'Natal dispersal in ravens is variable but can be substantial. Ringing and radio-tracking studies show that some juveniles remain within 50–100 km of their birth site, while others disperse much further. In European studies, dispersal distances of 200–500 km have been recorded for young ravens. In North America, ravens banded in one state have been recovered thousands of kilometres away. Dispersal appears to be influenced by habitat quality and local population density.' },
  { q: 'What is a group of young ravens called?', a: 'A group of ravens has several collective nouns in English: an "unkindness", a "conspiracy", or simply a "flock". The collective noun "unkindness" is frequently cited but historically informal. Groups of non-breeding young ravens in communal foraging and roosting contexts are simply called flocks or communal roosts in the scientific literature. These flocks can number from dozens to hundreds of birds in areas of concentrated food resources.' },
];
